00:12:55,440 --> 00:12:57,240
Mmm, smells great.

249
00:12:57,280 --> 00:12:59,700
George, seriously, what are you doing here?

250
00:13:00,320 --> 00:13:02,880
I haven't seen you since our divorce was finalized.

251
00:13:02,880 --> 00:13:04,080
You've got no right to be here.

252
00:13:04,360 --> 00:13:05,200
You're hardly homeless.

253
00:13:05,860 --> 00:13:07,060
Don't be like that, Judy.

254
00:13:07,520 --> 00:13:08,840
I really miss your home cooking.

255
00:13:15,760 --> 00:13:16,540
Alright, we're back.

256
00:13:16,800 --> 00:13:19,940
So talking about volunteering, have you ever done any volunteer work?

257
00:13:20,140 --> 00:13:20,380
I have.

258
00:13:20,540 --> 00:13:21,400
I've done quite a bit, actually.

259
00:13:22,080 --> 00:13:23,080
Yeah?

260
00:13:23,080 --> 00:13:24,080
What have you done?

261
00:13:24,080 --> 00:13:30,060
Back when I was living in the United States, in Chicago, I used to cook for a homeless shelter every week.

262
00:13:30,520 --> 00:13:31,660
So I would bring food every Thursday.

263
00:13:32,880 --> 00:13:34,820
So you cook at home and you bring the food over to the shelter?

264
00:13:35,200 --> 00:13:36,200
That's right.

265
00:13:36,200 --> 00:13:41,840
So the shelter had a kitchen, but they used donations from people because there are so many people who came there for food.

266
00:13:41,840 --> 00:13:44,260
And once a month I would volunteer to also serve the food.

267
00:13:45,160 --> 00:13:48,240
So I would bring lasagna or pasta, things that most people like.

268
00:13:48,720 --> 00:13:51,140
And then once a month I would sit down with people and talk.

269
00:13:51,360 --> 00:13:58,680
You know, a lot of people are lonely when they're in homeless shelters because they come alone or maybe it's the mother and her child.

270
00:13:58,680 --> 00:14:00,560
It's nice to also come and sit and talk to them.

271
00:14:01,290 --> 00:14:10,320
No, it's very interesting and I think volunteering is a much better solution than, for example, just giving out money to poor people or something like that.

272
00:14:10,320 --> 00:14:11,480
I don't think that's really the solution.

273
00:14:11,480 --> 00:14:16,760
I think you should dedicate your time and your energy to making a difference, like in the dialogue.

274
00:14:16,760 --> 00:14:24,370
And I think that the other benefit is for you, the volunteer, because it changes your perspective on poverty and on humanity.

275
00:14:24,960 --> 00:14:27,250
And you think about, well, you know, I could be this person.

276
00:14:27,700 --> 00:14:30,310
He was a banker two years ago and now he doesn't have a house.

277
00:14:30,880 --> 00:14:33,670
And so I think it has a positive effect on everybody involved.

278
00:14:34,070 --> 00:14:34,670
Yeah, for sure.

279
00:14:34,870 --> 00:14:36,530
No, I think it's a very rewarding experience.

280
00:14:36,530 --> 00:14:42,610
And I think EnglishPod definitely supports that you should volunteer, you should make a difference.

281
00:14:43,160 --> 00:14:45,600
And I think we can all help each other, right?

282
00:14:45,600 --> 00:14:45,730
That's right.

283
00:14:46,600 --> 00:14:47,630
Have you ever volunteered?

284
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:49,390
What kind of volunteer work have you done?

285
00:14:49,590 --> 00:14:51,170
Has it been environmental or social?

286
00:14:51,840 --> 00:14:53,760
Let us know on our website, EnglishPod.com.

287
00:14:53,760 --> 00:14:54,350
We hope to see you there.

288
00:14:54,530 --> 00:14:54,690
All right.

289
00:14:55,050 --> 00:14:55,290
Bye, everyone.

290
00:14:55,570 --> 00:14:55,690
Bye.
